course: dictionary
lesson: 2217
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. Look at the words around the word. «The main course was pasta.» Here course is a part of a meal, not a school subject.
explain: A course can be a set of lessons, like «a swimming course», or the path something follows, like a ship's course. Ask: what word would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: «Of course» is a phrase that means yes, certainly, or as you would expect. In «Tears coursed down her cheeks», course is a verb that means to flow fast. The sentence tells you this, because tears do not take lessons.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A course can be a set of lessons on a subject, such as «a swimming course».
fact: A course can be a part of a meal, such as «The main course was pasta.»
fact: A course can be an area for a sport or race, such as a golf course or an obstacle course.
fact: «Of course» means yes, certainly, or as you would expect. To course means to flow fast.
exercise: «The main course was pasta.» What is a course in this sentence? | *A part of a meal | A river path | A set of lessons | A race track
exercise: «Tears coursed down her cheeks.» What does coursed mean? | Was taught | Stopped suddenly | Ran a race | *Flowed fast
exercise: Lily asks, «Can I come too?» Her dad says, «Of course.» What does he mean? | Maybe later, if we have time | *Yes, certainly | No way | He has lost his keys
test: «The children ran around the obstacle course on sports day.» What is an obstacle course? | A meal with three parts | *A race area with barriers to get over | A river that bends | Lessons about cooking
test: «Ships can change course when the weather turns.» What does change course mean? | Stop for lunch | *Change the direction they travel in | Paint the ship a new colour | Sink to the bottom
exam: «Growing up brings big choices, such as which exams and courses to take.» What is a course in this sentence? | A river that bends | A race track | A part of a meal | *A set of lessons on a subject
